<div className="blog-faq">
<div className="phase-label" style={{ marginBottom: 20 }}>Frequently Asked Questions</div>
<div className="blog-faq-item">
<div className="blog-faq-q">Do you need an MBA to start a search fund?</div>
<p className="blog-faq-a">No, but it’s the norm in traditional, investor-funded search funds: 71% of searchers outside the US and Canada have one (IESE, 2026). Without one, expect more work to win investors’ trust before you have a deal.</p>
</div>
<div className="blog-faq-item">
<div className="blog-faq-q">Can you start a search fund without an MBA?</div>
<p className="blog-faq-a">Yes. About 4 in 10 of the newest international searchers in IESE’s 2026 study don’t have one. Self-funded searches and SBA-backed purchases are the easiest routes, because lenders and sellers judge experience, not degrees.</p>
</div>
<div className="blog-faq-item">
<div className="blog-faq-q">Do search fund investors require an MBA?</div>
<p className="blog-faq-a">Not formally, but many prefer one, and Mark Fleming says some funds only back Harvard and Stanford graduates. In Yale’s 2026 study, non-MBA-led search fund companies returned far less, from a sample of only 14.</p>
</div>
<div className="blog-faq-item">
<div className="blog-faq-q">Does an MBA improve search fund returns?</div>
<p className="blog-faq-a">MBA-led search funds have done better: a median IRR of about 28% against 1% for non-MBA-led ones in Yale’s 2026 study. Its authors say that doesn’t prove the degree is the cause.</p>
</div>
<div className="blog-faq-item">
<div className="blog-faq-q">What experience do you need to buy a business with an SBA loan?</div>
<p className="blog-faq-a">Lenders want direct industry or solid management experience, good credit and enough cash; no degree is required. Mark Fleming looks for 10 to 15 years of work, 5 managing or selling, and industry experience.</p>
</div>
<div className="blog-faq-item">
<div className="blog-faq-q">What percentage of searchers have an MBA?</div>
<p className="blog-faq-a">71% of searchers outside the US and Canada, and 60% of the newest cohort, according to IESE’s 2026 study, down from 89% in its 2016 study.</p>
</div>
</div>

